    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait why did u say 'gobbling up' lol. that sounds a bit weird. can u just explain exactly what happens to my earnest money once it's in their hands? like who actually controls it and when do they give it to the seller?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I guess I was just trying to \"gobble up\" the explanation in a hurry. Anyway, let's get down to business.\n\nWhen you deposit your earnest money into the escrow account, it's essentially a deposit into a separate, interest-bearing account that's held in trust for the transaction. The escrow agent acts as the custodian of this account, and they're required to follow strict guidelines to ensure that the funds are handled properly.\n\nIn most cases, the escrow agent will hold the earnest money until the following conditions are met:\n\n1. The inspection period: This is the time frame during which you can back out of the deal if you're not satisfied with the property's condition.\n2. The financing contingency: This is the time frame during which you're waiting to hear back from your lender about your loan approval.\n3. The closing date: This is the final date when the property is transferred to you, and the seller receives the payment.\n\nOnce these conditions are met, the escrow agent will release the earnest money to the seller, minus any applicable fees and deductions. Think of it like a \"gobbling up\" of the funds, but in a controlled and regulated environment.\n\nThe escrow agent is essentially a",
